description.narrative:
The programs expected impact is: Reduced multi-dimensional poverty in Zambia.
The programs key outcome is: Percentage of households in extreme poverty that are covered by at least one Social Protection programme (male/female) Through a financial contribution and technical assistance to Zambias national Social Protection system, the program will promote expanded coverage to support poor and extreme poor households, prioritising households at risk of climate disasters, ensuring gender and disability integration and contribute to increase the effectiveness and efficiency of the of social protection instruments, delivery systems to increase impact.
The three proposed key outputs for the program are:
(1) Provision of rights-based social protection enabled through: Systems Strengthening: Enhance institutional capacity for dynamic programme enrolment and delivery, cross-sectoral referrals, integrated social protection support, and improved accountability to beneficiaries;
Lifecycle-Based SP: Lay the groundwork for a shift to lifecycle-based Social Protection by conceptualization of the package of lifecycle entitlements, assessing current programme coverage, shifting from household focus to individual focus, better mainstreaming of disability, and embedding climate resilience considerations throughout;
Evidence-Based Programming: Improve monitoring and evaluation through harmonized systems and generate evidence to inform priority areas across all programmes to support GRZ to make informed programmatic decisions and reforms to achieve its policy objectives;
Enabling Environment: Support sustainable financing, strengthened coordination, and establish a more supportive legislative basis for Social Protection.
The revised National Social Protection Policy envisions a sustainable, integrated and responsive system throughout the lifecycle. The progra will help GRZ to realize this vision by building on progress made through the SCT. Consolidating gains achieved in the SCT, the program will shift focus toward enhancing the broader social protection system
(2) Leveraging Social Protection for climate change adaptation and food and nutrition security through:
Strengthening Shock-Responsiveness, including Anticipatory Action: Enhance the protective capacity of the social protection system by strengthening coordination between humanitarian and social protection sector and building the mechanisms for anticipatory action, enabling pre-emptive and early interventions to mitigate the impact of shocks;
Climate-Smart Linkages and Graduation Pathways: Systematically linking FSP and SCT households with persons with disabilities to existing productive opportunities and climate-resilient livelihood resources for enhanced productivity and food security.
The second output will ensure that social protection not only lifts households out of poverty but also equips them to withstand the crises and continue building resilience through shocks. By providing timely and accurate information on climate-associated risks, communities can be empowered to navigate and sustain shocks.
(3) Gender-responsive and disability-inclusive Social Protection, through:
Enhancing Gender-Responsiveness of Social Protection: Support GRZ to identify entry points and concrete opportunities to improve gender-responsiveness of the social protection system;
Strengthening Cash Plus for Disability Inclusion: Improve access of persons with disabilities to adequate SP and key supportive services, with the ultimate goal of facilitating independent living for persons with disabilities.
Recognizing systemic inequalities that disproportionately affect women and girls, as well as environmental and social barriers which prevent persons with disabilities fully participating in society, the program will support GRZ to mainstream gender and disability in Social Protection programming as reflected as a stated priority in the revised National Social Protection Policy and Implementation Plan
